  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Contractor assurance covers three things: competence checks, site induction records, and supply-chain scheme evidence</li><li>Under the Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, the client must take reasonable steps to satisfy itself that appointees are competent</li><li>Supply-chain assurance schemes such as SSIP, CHAS, Constructionline and Achilles pre-qualify contractors, but the certificate still has to be checked and current at the point of work</li><li>The check often happens but leaves no record; paper induction folders are not searchable and are invisible to the office</li><li>Quickler captures the competence check and induction at the gate and holds a live, searchable record across sites; it does not decide competence, a competent person does</li></ul></div><h2>What a contractor compliance audit is for</h2><p>A contractor compliance audit is the gate check: before a contractor or their workers start on a site, the organisation confirms they are competent, insured, inducted to the site rules, and cleared to do the specific work. It ties together three strands: the competence check, the site induction, and the supply-chain assurance evidence that pre-qualified the firm.</p><p>A signed induction folder does not prove any of this on its own. The checks actually happening, and being recorded so they can be produced later, is what proves it. Software makes the gate check easy to complete on site, harder to skip, and faster to surface when a certificate lapses. A good tool means the missing check is not discovered after an incident, when it is too late. This is not legal advice.</p><h2>The three strands of contractor assurance</h2><h3>1. Competence under CDM 2015</h3><p>Under the Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, the client must take reasonable steps to satisfy itself that those it appoints have the skills, knowledge, experience and organisational capability to do the work safely. That means checking cards and tickets, insurance, qualifications and method statements before work starts, and keeping the evidence.</p><h3>2. Site induction</h3><p>A site induction covers the site rules, hazards, welfare and emergency arrangements, and any permits or exclusion zones. The record has to be attributable: it must show that this named person was inducted, when, and by whom. A tick on a sheet in a folder is impossible to rely on later.</p><h3>3. Supply-chain scheme evidence</h3><p>Many organisations pre-qualify contractors through schemes. The Safety Schemes in Procurement (SSIP) forum lets member schemes recognise each other's assessments. CHAS, Constructionline and Achilles are widely used. Membership pre-qualifies the firm, but the certificate still has to be current at the point of work.</p><h2>Why the gate check still matters after pre-qualification</h2><p>Scheme membership and a stored insurance certificate pre-qualify a contractor firm, which is useful, but they do not remove the gate check. The certificate can lapse. The individual worker who turns up may not hold the ticket the task needs. The person may not have been inducted to this site. The gate check confirms the person and the currency of the evidence on the day, which is what an incident investigation or a client assurance audit actually asks about.</p><h2>What to look for in a contractor compliance app</h2><ul><li><strong>Capture at the gate:</strong> the tool should make it easy to record the check and induction as the contractor arrives, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Photo of the card or certificate:</strong> tied to the named person and a timestamp, not a folder sorted out later</li><li><strong>Expiry visibility:</strong> the office should see at a glance which certificates and scheme memberships are current and which have lapsed</li><li><strong>No per-seat or per-contractor penalty:</strong> a rotating supply chain should not cost more to onboard. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free, so you only pay for who works</li><li><strong>A searchable, attributable record:</strong> named person, site, timestamp, inductor, for the client audit or incident investigation that surfaces later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the competence check and induction at the gate and holds the evidence in a searchable record. It does not decide whether a contractor is competent, run a scheme assessment, or clear a worker to start. A competent person makes those judgements; Quickler holds the evidence that they were made. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your sites.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a contractor compliance audit?</h3><p>It is the gate check that confirms a contractor and their workers are competent, insured, inducted to the site rules and cleared for the specific work before they start. It ties together competence checks, the site induction record, and the supply-chain assurance evidence that pre-qualified the firm. Options for running it range from paper folders and spreadsheets to conversation-based tools like quickler that capture the check at the gate over WhatsApp.</p><h3>What does CDM 2015 require for contractor competence?</h3><p>The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015 require the client to take reasonable steps to satisfy itself that those it appoints have the skills, knowledge, experience and organisational capability to do the work safely. That means checking and keeping evidence of cards, insurance, qualifications and method statements before work starts. Check the current CDM guidance and take your own competent advice; this is not legal advice.</p><h3>Does SSIP or CHAS membership mean I do not need a gate check?</h3><p>No. SSIP, CHAS, Constructionline and Achilles pre-qualify a contractor firm, which reduces duplicate assessment, but the certificate still has to be current at the point of work and the individual worker still has to be inducted and competent for the task. The scheme assesses the firm; the gate check confirms the person and the currency of the evidence on the day.</p><h3>Can I run contractor inductions over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's contractor workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The site team or contractor receives each step in their existing WhatsApp chat, records the check and induction with text, a voice note or a photo of the card, and the record generates automatically and appears on the office dashboard. No separate app or login is required, and quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/compliance-audit-software-uk.html\">Compliance audit software for multi-site organisations</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/permit-to-work-system-app-uk.html\">Permit to work system and audit app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/internal-audit-checklist-app-uk.html\">Internal audit checklist app (ISO 9001, 14001, 45001)</a></li></ul>",
